deep.reconstruction:
Context: a 1982 note (the reverse sides of these sheets are computer printouts dated 82.160, i.e. June 1982), so it predates Pursuing Stacks (begun February 1983). Grothendieck is building a Quillen-type closed model structure on a topos A. A generating set (TC)_0 ⊂ C_0 of trivial cofibrations sits inside a set of cofibrations. From it come classes C and TC (cofibrations, trivial cofibrations) and, by right lifting, F and TF (fibrations, trivial fibrations), with factorizations obtained by a small-object-type argument.

Item c), with the crossed-out start 'X→Y is ∈ W', states the characterization TC = C ∩ W̃ and TF = F ∩ W̃. Here W̃ is the class of composites f = p∘i with i ∈ TC and p ∈ TF; W̃ is a class of arrows of A, plausibly 'W̃ ⊂ Fl(A)'. This is the standard retract argument: if f = p∘i ∈ C, lift in the square to exhibit f as a retract of i, so f ∈ TC. Dually for TF.

The NB records what has been used:
- only small colimits/sums and fibre products in A, not that A is a topos;
- not yet the class W itself;
- only the symmetric lifting duality between the pairs (TC, F) and (C, TF);
- not the inclusion TC ⊂ C (equivalently TF ⊂ F), nor the way the classes were generated.
This is an axiomatic 'weak factorization system' bookkeeping remark.

Item d) is the compatibility of the model structure with the cartesian closed structure of the topos. For i: A→B and f: X→Y, let
α(i,f): Hom(B,X) → Hom(i,f) := Hom(B,Y) ×_{Hom(A,Y)} Hom(A,X)
be the map into internal Homs. Consider the conditions:
- (i) C and TC are stable under − × Z;
- (ii) F and TF are stable under Hom(Z, −);
- (iii) if i ∈ C and f ∈ F then α(i,f) ∈ F, and α(i,f) ∈ TF if moreover i ∈ TC or f ∈ TF.
The claim, with marginal 'under suitable assumptions', is that these are equivalent. (iii) is the adjoint (pullback-hom) form of Quillen's SM7 / the pushout-product axiom for a cartesian monoidal model category. (i) and (ii) are its special cases with A = ∅ or Y = terminal object.

The purpose is a cartesian closed model structure on a topos, generated by a set of trivial monomorphisms, as a homotopy model (modelizer).
